 TRST 541, TRST 542, or consent of instructor.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"76915","code":"A","typeCode":"ONL","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Online","typeCode":"ONL","days":["R"],"start":690,"end":830,"location":"n.a.","instructors":["Arzik Erzurumlu, O"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Translation and Interpreting major(s)."],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This is an advanced course in simultaneous interpretation from and into English. It continues the study begun in TRST 541 and prepares students to enter the interpreting profession, both as working interpreters, and as scholars of interpreting studies. The course will also include practice in sight interpreting."}]},{"id":"TRST 545","subject":"TRST","number":"545","title":"Writing for Translators","creditHours":"4 hours","minHours":4,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Prerequisite: Graduate standing or permission of instructor; six semesters of non-English language study, or equivalent competence.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"76916","code":"A","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["W"],"start":600,"end":740,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["Phillips, P"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This course explores writing in the context of translation and looks closely at the question of what strategies and practices translators need to implement to adapt to each new text they encounter. Readings of a theoretical nature and documents of a practical nature will be assigned. The types of texts examined fall into the following categories: social sciences, literary, technical, legal, and scientific. The English-language conventions of specific types of texts will be addressed. Each student will also develop strategies for translating from specific languages, as well as individual practices for improving reading and writing."},{"crn":"78441","code":"ONL","typeCode":"ONL","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Online","typeCode":"ONL","days":["W"],"start":600,"end":740,"location":"n.a.","instructors":["Phillips, P"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to MA:Trnsl & Intrp Online - UIUC."],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This course explores writing in the context of translation and looks closely at the question of what strategies and practices translators need to implement to adapt to each new text they encounter. Readings of a theoretical nature and documents of a practical nature will be assigned. The types of texts examined fall into the following categories: social sciences, literary, technical, legal, and scientific. The English-language conventions of specific types of texts will be addressed. Each student will also develop strategies for translating from specific languages, as well as individual practices for improving reading and writing."}]}]